<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The whole Windows installer.

    wix extension add -g WixToolset.UI.wixext/5.0.2
    wix build installer/aircast.wxs -arch x64 -ext WixToolset.UI.wixext \
      -d Ver=$V -d Stage=dist/aircast -o Quoise-$V-x64.msi
    wix msi validate -ice ICE105 Quoise-$V-x64.msi

  MSI rather than an NSIS .exe for one reason: <MajorUpgrade/> removes the
  previous version's exact file set. This bundle is several hundred GStreamer
  plugin DLLs, and a stale plugin left behind by a partial upgrade is a crash,
  not a leak.

  Per-machine, into %ProgramFiles%, which costs a UAC prompt at install time and
  buys the thing that actually matters: the application directory is not
  writable by the user. GStreamer loads its plugins from beside the exe, and
  Windows searches the application directory first — a per-user install into
  %LocalAppData% would let any code running as that user drop a DLL there and
  be loaded by us. docs/threat-model.md, T5.

  Stage is made absolute here rather than required absolute from the caller,
  because a relative Files/@Include is not resolved against the working
  directory: it is appended to the source path of the Directory it sits in, so
  -d Stage=dist/aircast was searched for under "Quoise\dist\aircast",
  found nothing, and shipped a 65 KB installer holding one executable. A harvest
  root that does not exist is warning WIX8601, not an error. Swapping -b for -d
  changed nothing — both values were relative. The MSI size floor in CI is what
  now catches an empty harvest.

  The exe is declared explicitly, and inside bin/, for two different reasons.
  Explicitly, because the Shortcut needs a File to point at and harvested files
  get generated ids nothing can reference; the Exclude then stops the harvest
  installing a second copy of it. Inside bin/, because Source only says where
  the bits come from: the install target is the parent Directory. Declared
  straight under INSTALLFOLDER it installed to the install root while the
  harvest recreated the staged tree and put all 500-odd DLLs in bin/, so the
  installed program died before main(): those DLLs are static imports, and the
  loader resolves them from the application directory. main.c also derives
  <root>/share/glib-2.0/schemas by walking up out of bin, which only works from
  bin. CI now extracts the built package and checks that layout — the smoke test
  only ever looked at the staged tree, which was always right.

  BinFolder and the bin the harvest generates for itself are two Directory rows
  resolving to one folder. That is cosmetic; it buys a name WorkingDirectory and
  a future RemoveFolder can refer to, which an inline subdirectory would not.

  UpgradeCode is generated once and committed forever. Changing it gives a user
  two side-by-side installations fighting over the plugin scan.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui">
  <?define StageRoot=$(sys.CURRENTDIR)$(var.Stage)?>

  <Package Name="Quoise"
           Manufacturer="napejoon"
           Version="$(var.Ver)"
           UpgradeCode="027C7C65-2D1E-488A-A147-552B9D981628"
           Scope="perMachine">

    <!-- AllowSameVersionUpgrades, because the default bound is what left two
         "Quoise" rows in Apps and Features. Left to itself WiX writes
         the detect-and-remove row as "older than ProductVersion, maximum
         excluded", and every build that is not a tag stamps 0.0.0: 0.0.0 is not
         older than 0.0.0, so FindRelatedProducts matched nothing, nothing was
         removed, and the new ProductCode, a fresh GUID on every build because
         the Package above declares none, registered as a second product beside
         the first. Two installations of a several-hundred-DLL GStreamer bundle
         fighting over the plugin scan is the thing this file chose MSI to avoid.
         The cost is ICE61, which exists to say "same-version upgrades are
         unusual"; it is not among the ICEs CI runs, and it is the expected
         answer here rather than a surprise.

         No Schedule attribute: the default afterInstallValidate removes the old
         product before the new files are laid down, and that is what makes a
         same-version upgrade safe. afterInstallExecute would leave MSI applying
         file-versioning rules between two file sets carrying identical
         versions, and it may decline to copy. -->
    <MajorUpgrade AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
                  DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of [ProductName] is already installed." />
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!-- Without this the package has no UI sequence at all: double-clicking it
         shows a progress bar and then nothing, which reads as "did that work?".
         WixUI_InstallDir is the smallest set of dialogs that still answers the
         two questions a user has — what am I agreeing to, and where is this
         going — and it is where the UAC prompt for a per-machine install makes
         sense, rather than arriving unannounced.

         It needs `wix extension add -g WixToolset.UI.wixext/5.0.2` and
         `-ext WixToolset.UI.wixext` on the build; a missing extension is a
         build error, so CI cannot silently go back to no wizard. -->
    <ui:WixUI Id="WixUI_InstallDir" InstallDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />
    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="$(sys.CURRENTDIR)installer\license.rtf" />

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles6432Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Quoise">
        <Directory Id="BinFolder" Name="bin">
          <Component>
            <File Id="AircastExe" Source="$(var.StageRoot)\bin\aircast-receiver.exe" />
            <Shortcut Directory="ShortcutFolder" Name="Quoise"
                      Target="[#AircastExe]" WorkingDirectory="BinFolder" />
            <RemoveFolder Id="CleanShortcutFolder" Directory="ShortcutFolder" On="uninstall" />
          </Component>
        </Directory>

        <!-- The Exclude is load-bearing: without it the harvest installs its own
             copy of the exe into the same directory, and two components owning
             one target file is an ICE30 error, not a duplicate-file warning. -->
        <Files Include="$(var.StageRoot)\**">
          <Exclude Files="$(var.StageRoot)\bin\aircast-receiver.exe" />
        </Files>
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Directory Id="ShortcutFolder" Name="Quoise" />
    </StandardDirectory>
  </Package>
</Wix>
